{"id":"9b1264ff-7005-4e98-9898-965a39dd0017","object":"chat.completion","created":1785011600,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case.get('text')\\n    passes = case.get('passes')\\n    max_rounds = case.get('max_rounds')\\n    if not isinstance(text, str) or not isinstance(passes, list) or not isinstance(max_rounds, int):\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid case structure')\\n    if max_rounds < 1:\\n        raise ValueError('max_rounds must be positive')\\n    for p in passes:\\n        if not all(isinstance(p.get(k), str) for k in ['name', 'old', 'new']):\\n            raise ValueError('Pass must have name, old, new as strings')\\n        if p['old'] == '':\\n            raise ValueError('Empty old string not allowed')\\n    current = text\\n    applications = []\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old == new:\\n                continue\\n            idx = current.find(old)\\n            if idx != -1:\\n                current = current[:idx] + new + current[idx + len(old):]\\n                applications.append(p['name'])\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': True, 'text': current}\\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': False, 'text': current}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-CR-01. The task is about running a pass schedule to fixed point. The case payload has: text (string), passes (list of objects with name, old, new), max_rounds (int). The function must apply passes in order, each pass replaces all occurrences of old with new? Or at most first eligible rewrite per sweep? The description: \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" That suggests in each pass, we find the first occurrence of old in the current text and replace it with new? But the fixture example shows text \"aaaa bb cc\", passes: halve-a: old \"aa\" -> new \"a\", drop-b: old \"bb\" -> new \"b\", drop-c: old \" cc\" -> new \"\" (note leading space). If we apply each pass at most one rewrite, we need to consider the order. In first round: apply halve-a: first occurrence of \"aa\" in \"aaaa bb cc\". The first \"aa\" is at index 0. Replace with \"a\", text becomes \"aa bb cc\". Then drop-b: first occurrence of \"bb\" in current text \"aa bb cc\" -> at index 3? Replace \"bb\" with \"b\", text \"aa b cc\". Then drop-c: first occurrence of \" cc\" (space+cc) in \"aa b cc\": at index 4? Replace \" cc\" with \"\", text \"aa b\". So after round 1, text = \"aa b\". Second round: halve-a: first \"aa\" at index 0 -> replace with \"a\", text \"a b\". drop-b: first \"bb\"? now text \"a b\" has no \"bb\", so unchanged. drop-c: first \" cc\"? no, unchanged. So round 2 changes: halve-a changes. Now text \"a b\". Third round: halve-a: first \"aa\"? none; drop-b: none; drop-c: none. So fixed point reached after 2 changes? The output should include applications list (list of pass names that changed the artifact in order), fixed_point boolean, text final. Also max_rounds: if fixed point not reached within max_rounds, stop and return fixed_point false? The description: \"Repeat full sweeps until none changes or the round budget is consumed.\" So we need to track rounds. Each round we apply all passes in order, recording names of those that changed. After a round, if no changes, fixed_point true. If we have done max_rounds rounds and still changes, stop and fixed_point false. Also note: \"At most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" So each pass does at most one replacement per full sweep (round). We need to find the first occurrence of old in current text and replace it with new. Important: The replacement should be done on the current text sequentially. Also, the old string may be empty? Not specified but likely not. We'll assume old is non-empty.\n\nImplementation: define solve(case). Get text, passes, max_rounds. Validate that text is string, passes is list with each having name, old, new strings, max_rounds is int >= 0? Actually should be positive. We'll raise ValueError for invalid missing fields.\n\nAlgorithm:\ncurrent_text = text\napplications = []  # list of pass names that have been applied in order across all rounds? Actually description: \"Record only passes that change the artifact.\" It says \"Return the artifact, application trace, and fixed-point flag.\" The output schema has applications as array of strings. Should be in order of application, perhaps across all sweeps. In example above, we would have: \"halve-a\", \"drop-b\", \"drop-c\" (round1), \"halve-a\" (round2) => [\"halve-a\", \"drop-b\", \"drop-c\", \"halve-a\"]. That seems plausible.\n\nBut need to check if we record each change individually per round. Yes.\n\nWe'll need to loop for round in range(max_rounds): changed_this_round = False; for each pass in passes: find first occurrence of pass['old'] in current_text; if found, replace that occurrence with pass['new']; record pass['name'] in applications; set changed_this_round = True. After loop, if not changed_this_round: fixed_point = True; break. If after max_rounds still changing, fixed_point = False.\n\nReturn {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": current_text}.\n\nNote: The first occurrence replacement: use str.find() and then slicing: index = current_text.find(old_str); if index != -1: current_text = current_text[:index] + new_str + current_text[index+len(old_str):]; Add name to applications.\n\nWe must not mutate input case; we are using its fields directly.\n\nValidation: Check that 'text' is string, 'passes' is list, each item has 'name', 'old', 'new' as strings, 'max_rounds' is int >= 1 maybe. Raise ValueError if missing.\n\nNow produce the JSON with implementation key.\n\nMake sure to escape properly. We'll write Python code and then JSON-encode it. We'll include necessary comments? Not required but can improve readability. We'll keep it simple.\n\nEdge cases: If old string is empty? Not defined, but find would always return 0 and cause infinite loop; we could treat as no replacement? Better to raise ValueError if old is empty. Also if new is same as old? Could cause infinite? Not, because we replace only first occurrence, still might not change? Actually if new == old, replacement does nothing but still considered a change? Should treat as no change? The description says \"eligible rewrite\" – if replacement yields same string, maybe it doesn't count as change? But we only record changes, and we can check if the replacement actually changes the text. Since we are replacing with same string, text unchanged, so we should not record it. So we can check if old != new before considering? But also if old == new, the replacement is no-op, so we can skip. However the problem might assume they are different. We'll check if old == new: skip. Also if old is empty, skip or raise? We'll raise ValueError for empty old.\n\nAlso ensure str.find works for empty? We'll raise.\n\nNow implement solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case.get('text')\n    passes = case.get('passes')\n    max_rounds = case.get('max_rounds')\n    if not isinstance(text, str) or not isinstance(passes, list) or not isinstance(max_rounds, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case structure\")\n    if max_rounds < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"max_rounds must be positive\")\n    for p in passes:\n        if not all(isinstance(p.get(k), str) for k in ['name','old','new']):\n            raise ValueError(\"Pass must have name, old, new strings\")\n        if p['old'] == '':\n            raise ValueError(\"Empty old string not allowed\")\n    current = text\n    applications = []\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            if old == new:\n                continue\n            idx = current.find(old)\n            if idx != -1:\n                current = current[:idx] + new + current[idx + len(old):]\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": True, \"text\": current}\n    return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": False, \"text\": current}\n\nWe used range(max_rounds). Note that if round consumes all rounds, we break after loop? Actually loop ends after max_rounds iterations; if still changes, return fixed_point False. If at any round no changes, return fixed_point True before finishing all rounds.\n\nNow write JSON with implementation string.\n\nMake sure to escape quotes, backslashes, newlines.\n\nThe response should be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". We'll output that.\n\nWe'll test with the fixture: text \"aaaa bb cc\", passes as given, max_rounds=8. Our algorithm: round1: halve-a at index0 -> \"aa bb cc\", drop-b at index3? Actually \"aa bb cc\" find \"bb\" at index3 -> \"aa b cc\", drop-c find \" cc\" at index4 -> \"aa b\". applications: [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\"]. round2: halve-a find \"aa\" at index0 -> \"a b\", drop-b no \"bb\", drop-c no \" cc\". applications add \"halve-a\". So total applications: 4, fixed_point true after round2. So output text \"a b\". That seems plausible.\n\nBut note the fixture payloads are all same, and the expected outputs are not given. We assume this is correct.\n\nAlso note: The case may have _case_nonce but we ignore it.\n\nWe'll produce the answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1310,"completion_tokens":2287,"total_tokens":3597,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1280},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1942},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1280,"prompt_cache_miss_